RUN: %COMPILE_IR %S/execution_order_line.cfc | %CHECK_IR %s

The diagram, with execution orders in parentheses:

a --+--> b (1)
    +--> c (2)

A debugger reads line N of the diagram's debug file as execution order N, so each
ordered element must get its execution order as its line, with no offset.

The stores are matched inside the function, so no other function's instructions can bind.
CHECK-LABEL: define void @execution_order_line(ptr %0) !dbg ![[#SCOPE_FUNCTION:]]
CHECK:       store i16 %{{.*}}, ptr %b, {{.*}}!dbg ![[#KEY_B:]]
CHECK:       store i16 %{{.*}}, ptr %c, {{.*}}!dbg ![[#KEY_C:]]

The diagram statements are scoped to a lexical block bound to the diagram's own file.
CHECK-DAG: ![[#FILE_DIAGRAM:]] = !DIFile(filename: "{{.*}}execution_order_line.cfc.execution_order_line", directory:
CHECK-DAG: ![[#SCOPE_DIAGRAM:]] = distinct !DILexicalBlock(scope: ![[#SCOPE_FUNCTION]], file: ![[#FILE_DIAGRAM]])

Each store is the key instruction of its element, the only place a debugger stops:
order 1 at line 1, order 2 at line 2.
CHECK-DAG: ![[#KEY_B]] = !DILocation(line: 1, scope: ![[#SCOPE_DIAGRAM]], atomGroup: [[#]], atomRank: 1)
CHECK-DAG: ![[#KEY_C]] = !DILocation(line: 2, scope: ![[#SCOPE_DIAGRAM]], atomGroup: [[#]], atomRank: 1)
